1: # Source-zero real trace and actual Cox divisor: working calculation
2: 
3: This note records exact local maps toward the remaining source-zero order-two
4: attachment. It does not assign either individual source-zero return. We retain
5: the physical line convention of P3K.68 and the literal maps of the current-run
6: source-zero completion note.
7: 
8: ## RT.1. The horizontal trace immersion
9: 
10: The marked small-resolution coordinates are
11: \[
12:  A=Dz,\qquad C=Bz,\qquad v=(B-Dz)/2.
13: \]
14: On the normalization of the selected nodal fibre,
15: \[
16:  X=s^2-1,\quad Y=s(s^2-1-v),\quad T=v^2-2v,\quad
17:  z=(s+1)/(s^2+s-v).
18: \]
19: Put \(x=s+1,v=xr\). The blowup chart has the exact map
20: \[
21:  z=(x-1-r)^{-1},\quad B=x(x-2)(x-1-r),\quad
22:  D=(B-2xr)/z.
23: \]
24: The Jacobian of \((z,B)\) with respect to \((x,r)\) is
25: \(-2(x-1)/(x-1-r)\), hence is nonzero on the exceptional curve wherever
26: this chart is finite. At its missing point use
27: \(w=x-1-r,C=x(x-2)\); the Jacobian is \(2x-2\), again a unit there.
28: The second blowup chart \(v=y,x=yh\) has
29: \(z=h/(-1-h+yh^2)\), whose h derivative at y=0 is
30: \(-1/(-1-h)^2\). These formulas give the actual immersion of the complete
31: horizontal normalization trace through the exceptional curve.
32: 
33: Let \(F=\operatorname{Bl}_{(-1,0)}(\mathbb P^1_s\times D_v)\) and let E
34: be its exceptional curve. The Cartier ratio
35: \((s-\sqrt{1+v})/(s+\sqrt{1+v})\) has divisor
36: \(D_+-D_--E\). The actual source line restricts as \(O(-aE)\).
37: Consequently the retained spinor determinant on this trace is
38: \((1-2a)E\); reversing the two selected branches gives
39: \(-(1+2a)E\). The local intersection numbers \(E^2=-1,\sigma(F)=-1\)
40: therefore give the two local physical index expressions
41: \[
42:  a(a-1)/2,\qquad a(a+1)/2.
43: \]
44: Their difference is a. This is an intersection calculation on the specified
45: trace. The complete global fixed/cap trace has not yet been identified with F,
46: so we do not use either expression as a global return.
47: 
48: ## RT.2. Exact q factorization on the Cox bridge
49: 
50: Use the actual Cox coordinates
51: \[
52:  A'=s_0t_0,\quad B'=s_1t_1,\quad C'=s_0t_1,\quad D'=s_1t_0,
53: \]
54: with physical weights \((-1,-1,+1,+1)\) on
55: \((s_0,s_1,t_0,t_1)\), divided by the retained \(\mu_a\) when appropriate.
56: The actual base functions, before any quotient completion, are
57: \[
58:  v=(s_1t_1-s_0t_0)/2,\qquad
59:  q=(s_1-s_0)(t_0+t_1)-(s_0t_1-v)^2.
60: \]
61: Set \(x=s_0,y=t_1,k=s_1-s_0,l=t_0+t_1\), and choose the analytic unit
62: \(\sigma=\sqrt{1+xy}\) with value 1 at the origin. Define
63: \[
64:  F_+=\frac{(\sigma+1)k-x^2l/(\sigma+1)}2,\qquad
65:  F_-=\frac{(\sigma+1)l-y^2k/(\sigma+1)}2.
66: \]
67: Then
68: \[
69:  q=F_+F_-,\qquad
70:  \det\frac{\partial(F_+,F_-)}{\partial(k,l)}=\sigma.
71: \]
72: Thus this is an actual analytic coordinate change over \((x,y)\), with inverse
73: \[
74:  k=\frac{(\sigma+1)F_++x^2F_-/(\sigma+1)}{2\sigma},\quad
75:  l=\frac{y^2F_+/(\sigma+1)+(\sigma+1)F_-}{2\sigma}.
76: \]
77: The weights of \(F_+,F_-\) are respectively \(-1,+1\). No physical
78: character is changed. The source parameter becomes
79: \[
80:  v=xy+\frac{yF_+-xF_-}{2\sigma}.
81: \]
82: On the chart with invariant coordinates \(u=x/F_+,z=y/F_-\), this reads
83: \[
84:  v=q\left(uz+\frac{z-u}{2\sqrt{1+uzq}}\right).
85: \]
86: These formulas retain both actual branches of q=0. They show why a proposed
87: completion has to inspect their quotient strata at v=0 explicitly; the
88: horizontal trace and the isolated origin link do not by themselves enumerate
89: all strata of such a completion.
90: 
91: ## RT.3. The local real chamber map
92: 
93: On the Cox cover define
94: \(\mu=(\lVert t\rVert^2-\lVert s\rVert^2)/2\). The positive real part of
95: the physical complex action is
96: \[
97:  (s,t)\longmapsto(R^{-1/a}s,R^{1/a}t),\qquad R>0.
98: \]
99: Its moment coordinate has strictly positive derivative on every nonzero orbit.
100: It therefore gives the unique transport between two prescribed moment levels
101: on an orbit that meets both levels. The negative and positive chambers give the
102: two displayed small resolutions. In the common Cox coordinates the actual
103: primitive source translation has identity line coordinate; this is exactly the
104: map \(U:(p,w)\mapsto(p-G,w/X(p-G))\) in the source divisor frames. Its a-th
105: power line version is retained on \(\mathbb C^4/\mu_a\).
106: 
107: The additional actual suspension factor is
108: \(2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda q\). Its positive norm and phase must both be included
109: when chamber pieces are attached. We have not extended this real map through
110: every q=0 quotient stratum or the finite logarithmic cap, so no global real
111: bordism is asserted here.
112: 
113: ## RT.4. A primary link-classification input and its exact remaining arrow
114: 
115: Skopenkov, *Suspension theorems for links and link maps*, arXiv
116: math/0610320v3, Theorem 1.1 and Corollary 3.2, gives the classification of
117: ordered smooth links \(S^2\sqcup S^2\subset S^5\). Haefliger, *Differentiable
118: embeddings of spheres*, Corollary 6.6 (printed p.422), gives
119: \(C_2^3=0\). The other possible summand is
120: \(\pi_1(SO/SO_2)=0\), by the exact sequence of
121: \(SO_2\to SO\to SO/SO_2\): the map on \(\pi_1\) is the surjection
122: \(\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Z/2\). Hence the link group is \(\mathbb Z\).
123: The integer is the oriented complement degree: Skopenkov's definition of
124: \(\lambda\) in §3, printed pp.9–10, is the actual retraction of the complement
125: onto a meridian \(S^2\). Corollary 3.2 provides the explicit right inverse
126: \(x\mapsto(x/2,\varphi(x))\) in \(D^3\times S^2\), where
127: \(\deg\varphi\) is that integer.
128: 
129: This is a complete classification of the stated embedded two-sphere link.
130: For its use here, the actual source vanishing cycle must first be identified
131: with the circle lift of the second sphere, including its normal framing and
132: the finite-cap comparison. The local Cox quotient has circle degree a on that
133: second sphere. That fact supplies its local Euler evaluation; it does not yet
134: supply the required global embedding/framing arrow.
135: 
